---
/** The eight layers, as in README.md "Why it is safe". Keep the two in step. */
---

<table class="layers">
	<caption>
		Each layer is enough for the job it has; all of them run together. The role's grants are the
		wall. The others are fences: none stops a write by itself.
	</caption>
	<thead>
		<tr><th scope="col">Layer</th><th scope="col">What it does</th></tr>
	</thead>
	<tbody>
		<tr>
			<th scope="row">DB role</th>
			<td>
				<code>SELECT</code> on visible columns only, granted table by table, and no write privilege
				anywhere. PII and secret columns are hidden at the Postgres level, so <code>SELECT *</code> on
				those tables fails; views that would expose one are not granted. Defaults:
				<code>default_transaction_read_only</code>, 10 s statement timeout, 1 s lock timeout; 5
				connections. The role can change its own defaults in a direct session;
				<code>daypass doctor</code> reports drift.
			</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<th scope="row">No stored password</th>
			<td>
				On AWS RDS the role logs in with a 15-minute IAM token minted from <em>your</em> AWS
				identity. Lose AWS access, lose Daypass access.
			</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<th scope="row">Real parser</th>
			<td>
				Every statement goes through Postgres's own parser (<code>libpg-query</code>). One
				statement of at most 256 KiB; <code>SELECT</code> or <code>EXPLAIN</code> only; no data-modifying CTE, <code>FOR UPDATE</code>, <code>SELECT INTO</code>, <code>EXPLAIN ANALYZE</code>, <code>pg_sleep</code>, <code>dblink</code>, <code>pg_read_file</code>, <code>set_config</code>, <code>nextval</code>, advisory locks, <code>query_to_xml</code>, … The block list cannot cover every extension; the role's <code>EXECUTE</code> grants are the boundary there.
			</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<th scope="row">Read-only transaction</th>
			<td>
				Each call runs in <code>BEGIN READ ONLY</code> with a timeout of 10 s or the lease time
				left, whichever is shorter, and the session is reset afterwards. A call that is cut off is
				cancelled in the database too.
			</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<th scope="row">Result caps</th>
			<td>
				500 rows and 1 MB per result, cut in the database before the rows are sent; 2000
				characters per cell; 32 MiB on the wire per call.
			</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<th scope="row">Audit</th>
			<td>
				Two JSON lines per call in <code>~/.daypass/audit.jsonl</code>, one before the query runs
				and one when it ends: time, caller (AWS ARN for IAM targets), lease id, reason, SQL, rows,
				ms, error. Refusals get one line with the reason.
			</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<th scope="row">Network</th>
			<td>
				Optional: punch your current IP into the DB security group for the lease; removed on
				close, on the first tool call after expiry, or by <code>daypass sweep</code>.
			</td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<th scope="row">TLS</th>
			<td>Always verified. RDS targets (IAM, or any <code>*.rds.amazonaws.com</code> host) pin the bundled AWS RDS CA.</td>
		</tr>
	</tbody>
</table>

<style>
	.layers {
		width: 100%;
		max-width: 56rem;
		border-collapse: collapse;
		font-size: 0.925rem;
		line-height: 1.55;
	}
	caption {
		caption-side: top;
		text-align: left;
		color: var(--land-muted);
		padding-bottom: 1rem;
	}
	th,
	td {
		text-align: left;
		vertical-align: top;
		padding: 0.75rem 0.75rem 0.75rem 0;
		border-bottom: 1px solid var(--sl-color-hairline-light);
		overflow-wrap: anywhere;
	}
	thead th {
		font-size: 0.8rem;
		text-transform: uppercase;
		letter-spacing: 0.04em;
		color: var(--land-muted);
		font-weight: 600;
	}
	tbody th {
		color: var(--sl-color-white);
		font-weight: 600;
		white-space: nowrap;
		width: 1%;
		padding-right: 1.25rem;
	}
	td:last-child {
		padding-right: 0;
	}
	@media (max-width: 30em) {
		tbody th {
			white-space: normal;
			min-width: 6rem;
		}
	}
</style>
